37.8.m:
'Move forward, O lord of the world,' [he says];
for he is the lord of beings.
'Unto all domains,' [he says];
all domains are indeed the deities;
unto the deities alone he causes him to move forward.
'Having become an eagle, fly away,' [he says];
the eagle is indeed the swiftest of birds;
having made him an eagle, he delivers him beyond the rakṣases.
'Prepared in our sacrificer's house,' [he says];
he makes him having the sacrificer's [home] as his abode;
moreover, in the sacrificer's house itself he makes him prepared with the deities.
'Homage to the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa,' [he says];
having become Varuṇa, this one approaches the sacrificer;
he makes him Mitra.
They lift up the throne;
just as they lift up a throne for a human king who has arrived, even so is this.
With Agni he establishes himself;
Agni is all the deities;
with the deities alone he establishes him.
At that time, Agni and Soma aim at the sacrificer;
when he establishes [himself] with an animal,
that one he assigns to them both.
He who is consecrated indeed offers himself for the sacrifice to Agni and Soma;
when he offers the animal,
by that he redeems himself.
One that is hornless, bearded, having the form of two deities, hairy, fat, having the form of a man—such a one he should offer.
He who eats of it eats a man;
therefore one should not eat of it.
[The consecrated one indeed starves;]
[this one is offered as a redemption from hunger;]
[he who eats of it becomes hungry;]
[therefore one should not] eat [of it].
By the vigor of Agni and Soma, Indra slew Vṛtra;
this animal of Vṛtra-slaying nature is offered for victory;
therefore one should eat of it.
The deities indeed did not move forward to him when he was being offered on the previous day, [thinking,] 'We might be excluded from the subsequent sacrificial rite.'
Agni and Soma alone moved forward to him; turning away, the other [deities] let go of the sacrifice.
Therefore this animal dedicated to Agni and Soma is offered;
therefore a cup is not drawn for Agni and Soma.
Those two, returning again to the gods, laid down what was their dear domain;
the gods resolved to search for it;
they went seeking it with outstretched arms;
they went down [thinking], 'Freshly crept has this [fluid] been';
because they said 'fresh' (navam),
that is the butter-ness of fresh butter (navanīta);
because [they said] 'crept' (sṛptam), that is the clarified butter-ness of clarified butter (sarpiṣ);
because the goat (aja) found it, that is the ghee-ness of ghee (ājya);
he bleated 'ghṛṅ';
that is the clarified butter-ness of clarified butter (ghṛta);
therefore the goat goes making the sound 'ghṛṅ';
they reproduced that in the cattle;
the cattle brought that together;
by that, by this, they are born from cattle;
those two said: 'Let us choose a boon: let this one be our share, who found our dear domain';
therefore this animal dedicated to Agni and Soma is offered;
therefore one should eat of it;
for it was chosen as a boon.
